   "t": "p",
   "text": "Instead of writing `match` every time there are handy methods: `.has_value()` tells whether there is a value, `.value_or(default)` gives the default when there is none, and `.value_or_panic()` unwraps the value when you are sure it exists (otherwise the program crashes; use it only in tests and prototypes)."
  },
  {
   "t": "code",
   "code": [
    "fn find_index(items: []i32, target: i32) -> Optional[usize]:",
    "    for idx, val in items:",
    "        if val == target:",
    "            return Valid(idx)",
    "    return None",
    "",
    "fn main():",
    "    numbers: [4]i32 = [10, 25, 40, 85]",
    "    print(find_index(numbers, 25).has_value())",
    "    print(find_index(numbers, 99).has_value())",
    "    print(find_index(numbers, 99).value_or(0))",
    "    print(find_index(numbers, 25).value_or_panic())"
   ],
   "run": true,
   "output": [
    "true",
    "false",
    "0",
    "1"
   ]
